//
// The rubble layer — what RUNTIME has piled on top of the map.
//
// Plan 11 phase F6 built it; plan 12 B1 named it.  A pile of bodies
// and a heap of broken wall are one thing wearing two words: both are
// runtime debris that raises a hex, is climbable when it is low
// enough, and can be cleared back to the ground it fell on.  So this
// file gained a **source** (what the pile is made of) and a **clear**
// (the player's counter-play) rather than a second sparse map beside
// it.
//
// `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Where `height` comes from names three
// sources, and they are three different KINDS of state:
//
//   | source     | value                    | state              |
//   |------------|--------------------------|--------------------|
//   | structures | `height_override`        | authored — palette |
//   | terrain    | the slope solver         | derived — plan 02  |
//   | bodies     | accumulated pile height  | **runtime**        |
//
// This file is the third row and nothing else.  It is a sparse map of
// metres ADDED to whatever the palette already puts on a hex, so a
// pile on grass and a pile on a wall are the same arithmetic and
// neither is a special case.
//
// ── Why it is not a field on `PaintedWorld` ─────────────────────
//
// `PaintedWorld` is AUTHORED data: it is what the editor writes and
// what `save.loft` persists.  A body pile is none of those things —
// [`plans/07`](../plans/07-shared-world-substrate/README.md)
// § Evaluated sorts state as *authored → a layer, derived →
// recompute, runtime → sim state*, and plan 11 says it directly:
// **pile heights live with the wave, not in the save.**  So this
// layer rides on `WaveState`, is rebuilt with every wave, and never
// reaches a `.json`.
//
// ── Why F6 builds it before anything drops a body ───────────────
//
// F6's gate is *"the same predicate re-run with a raised hex must
// flip who can pass"*.  Nothing can flip if height is read straight
// off the palette — a class table that only reads materials answers
// the same thing forever, which is exactly the implementation plan 11
// § F0 point 3 warned about.  The layer is what makes the height rule
// a rule about the WORLD rather than about the ground types, and it
// is measurable today with `height_raise` standing in for the combat
// that will fill it.
//
// ── A RISE, not an absolute height ──────────────────────────────
//
// A miss is 0.0 m — "nothing has been piled here" — and that is the
// sparse default doing the right thing rather than an accident.  An
// absolute-height layer would need every painted hex present before
// it meant anything, and an absent entry would read as *sea level*
// under a 3 m wall.  Storing the rise means the empty layer is
// exactly "the map as painted", which is what every scene that has
// not killed anything yet needs.


// ── An entry means a PILE, and that is an invariant ─────────────
//
// A hex is in the hash only while it carries rubble.  Shrinking a pile
// to nothing removes it, exactly as `painted.loft` removes a hex
// painted back to sea — so "is there rubble here?" is one question
// with one answer, and `height_count` is the number of piles rather
// than the number of hexes that have ever had one.
//
// ⚠ That invariant is what `passable.loft` reads.  A piled hex's
// SURFACE is rubble, so a zeroed-but-present entry would leave an
// enemy standing on debris that is not there — and over water, where
// plan 12 B2 breaks a wall, that is the difference between a breach
// and a hole in the sea.
//
// ── The source is one per hex, and the newest lies on top ───────
//
// `plans/12` § The rubble is the hill draws the line this file sits
// on: **source material** — wreckage, carapace, masonry — is a closed
// set with one value per hex, exactly like `sand` / `grass` / `rock`.
// What a pile CONTAINS — several salvageable types at once — is open
// and multiple per hex, so it is not a ground type and not this
// layer's; it waits for plan 06 S1's stacked layer.
//
// B1 ships ONE rubble ground type, so nothing reads the source back
// yet.  It is stored anyway because the producers are already three —
// a wall breaking, a robot dying, an insect dying — and they arrive in
// B2 and B4: taking it as an argument now makes the eventual split a
// palette row and a lookup, rather than a hunt through every site that
// makes rubble.


// What a pile is MADE of.  A closed set, one value per hex.
//
// New variants APPEND — the numbers ride in the layer, and a run in
// progress must not be re-labelled underneath itself.
pub const RUBBLE_WRECKAGE: integer = 0;   // a machine that died
pub const RUBBLE_CARAPACE: integer = 1;   // an insect that died
pub const RUBBLE_MASONRY:  integer = 2;   // a structure that broke

// A harvester that died — a machine, plus **what it was hauling**
// (plan 23 K0).  `DESIGN.md` § Small robots gives the hauler its one
// mechanical distinction as *what it CARRIES*, and this is the whole
// of that mechanism: the body is a distinct source, and
// `wallet.loft::loot_rate` already prices a source.
//
// ⚠ **So the harvester is a design decision that cost no new
// behaviour** — the two lookups it needs (`damage.loft::body_source`
// and `loot_rate`) were both written as tables years of phases before
// anything varied in them.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A pile is named by its NEWEST deposit** (§ The source is one
// per hex), so a robot dying on top of a harvester's cargo devalues
// the whole heap to plain wreckage — and the reverse enriches it.
// That is the existing one-source-per-hex simplification showing
// through a much sharper number than it used to: with wreckage and
// carapace both at 20 pt/m nobody could see it, and at 60 a player
// can.  It is not a bug this plan introduces; it is plan 06 S1's
// stacked layer earning its place.
pub const RUBBLE_CARGO:    integer = 3;   // a hauler died with a load on

// The source's name, for a message a player or a failing script can
// read.  An unknown source names its number rather than guessing at a
// material nobody chose — the same rule
// `passable.loft::enemy_class_name` follows.
pub fn rubble_source_name(source: u8) -> text {
    if source == RUBBLE_WRECKAGE as u8 { return "wreckage"; }
    if source == RUBBLE_CARAPACE as u8 { return "carapace"; }
    if source == RUBBLE_MASONRY  as u8 { return "masonry"; }
    if source == RUBBLE_CARGO    as u8 { return "cargo"; }
    "source {source as integer}"
}

// One hex, how far runtime has raised it in metres, and what the pile
// is made of.
pub struct HexRise {
    q:      integer,
    r:      integer,
    metres: float,
    source: u8,
}

// What runtime has piled on the map.  A hash must be a struct field
// (the same loft idiom `PaintedWorld` and `Occupancy` follow), and
// wrapping it gives the "a miss is 0.0" rule one door rather than
// every caller doing its own null arithmetic — the same reason
// `flow_distance` is the field's only door.
pub struct HeightLayer {
    cells: hash<HexRise[q, r]>,
}

// Factory: the map exactly as painted.
//
// What every caller with no bodies on the ground passes, and it means
// "nothing has been piled", never "skip the height rule".
pub fn height_layer_empty() -> HeightLayer {
    HeightLayer { cells: [] }
}

// Pile `metres` more of `source` onto (q, r).
//
// ⚠ It ACCUMULATES, because bodies do (`ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Bodies
// are terrain: *"a body raises its hex's effective height, and bodies
// accumulate"*).  A setter would make the second death on a hex
// overwrite the first, and the pile that closes a chokepoint would
// never grow past one body.
//
// ⚠ **The pile has a CEILING as well as a floor**, and B0 measured it:
// a single-hex ramp onto a structure `H` high needs a climb of `H / 2`,
// so the workable pile heights are the band `[H - c, c]`.  Three robot
// bodies on one hex is a 3.0 m step and a 2.0 m climber cannot get onto
// its own ramp any more.  Nothing here enforces that — it falls out of
// the height rule — but a caller that expects "more bodies is always
// more ramp" is wrong about the mechanic.
//
// A negative rise is how a pile SHRINKS: the player collecting bodies
// is the counter-play the same section describes.  Taking away as much
// as fell REMOVES the pile rather than leaving a 0.0 m one — see
// § An entry means a PILE — and taking away more digs no hole.
//
// The source of a shrink is ignored: what is carried off does not
// change what the rest of the heap is made of.
pub fn height_raise(hl: HeightLayer, q: integer, r: integer, metres: float, source: u8) {
    hr_c = hl.cells[q, r];
    hr_total = metres;
    if hr_c != null { hr_total = hr_c.metres + metres; }

    if hr_total <= 0.0 {
        hl.cells[q, r] = null;
    } else if hr_c == null {
        hl.cells[q, r] = HexRise { q: q, r: r, metres: hr_total, source: source };
    } else {
        hr_c.metres = hr_total;
        // The newest thing to fall lies on top, and the source is one
        // per hex — so a deposit renames the pile and a withdrawal
        // leaves the name alone.
        if metres > 0.0 { hr_c.source = source; }
    }
}

// Take the whole pile away, leaving the hex exactly as the map paints
// it.
//
// ⚠ **This is what makes the rubble a LAYER rather than a repaint**,
// and it is the invariant plan 12 B1 gates: the ground underneath was
// never overwritten, so clearing restores what was authored with
// nothing to reconstruct.  Had a broken wall repainted its hex, clear
// would have had to remember a kind that the painted layer — sparse
// and sea-default — could not have told it.
//
// Clearing an unpiled hex is a no-op rather than an error: the player
// sweeping ground that turned out to be clean has done nothing wrong.
pub fn height_clear(hl: HeightLayer, q: integer, r: integer) {
    hl.cells[q, r] = null;
}

// How far (q, r) has been raised above what the palette paints there.
//
// ⚠ **The only door.**  Reading `hl.cells[q, r].metres` directly gives
// null on an unpiled hex, and loft's `?` turns that into 0 — which is
// the right answer here by luck rather than by rule.  Ask here, so the
// day a layer stores something whose zero is not its default, every
// caller already goes through one function.
pub fn height_rise(hl: const HeightLayer, q: integer, r: integer) -> float {
    hrs_c = hl.cells[q, r];
    if hrs_c == null { return 0.0; }
    hrs_c.metres
}

// Does (q, r) carry rubble at all?
//
// ⚠ **The question `passable.loft` asks**, and it is asked here rather
// than as `height_rise(...) > 0.0` at the call site, so that the
// entry-means-a-pile invariant has one reader.  A hex with a pile
// stands on RUBBLE; a hex without one stands on what the map paints.
pub fn height_piled(hl: const HeightLayer, q: integer, r: integer) -> boolean {
    height_rise(hl, q, r) > 0.0
}

// What the pile on (q, r) is made of — `-1` where there is no pile.
//
// The absent answer is a number outside the closed set rather than a
// material, following `bindings.loft::action_palette_index`: "no pile"
// must not read as "a pile of wreckage" at any call site.
pub fn height_source(hl: const HeightLayer, q: integer, r: integer) -> integer {
    hs_c = hl.cells[q, r];
    if hs_c == null { return -1; }
    hs_c.source as integer
}

// How many hexes carry a pile — the size of the layer.
pub fn height_count(hl: const HeightLayer) -> integer {
    len(hl.cells)
}
